markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Filtering in JavaScript

#### In the next level, you will use AI to help you apply a filter function to a community volunteer finder app. But first, let's take a look at what the `.filter()` function does. 

#### `.filter()` is an array method that makes a new list from an old list by keeping only the items that pass a test.

<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box;"> <p style="font-size: 14pt; margin: 0; line-height: 130%;">📖  <strong>.filter()</strong> loops through every item in an array and keeps it only if your function returns true.</p> </div>

## Starting with a full data set

<img src="https://images.code.org/07859f953499a6ef709e164b06c26367-image-1769452525453.png" alt="javascript of a database with volunteer opportunities in environment, education, and health" width="750px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

#### You have a full dataset, but the user doesn’t always want all roles. They might want:

- #### only **Health**

- #### only roles with **minAge <= 12**

- #### or both at the same time

#### So you use `.filter()` to create a filteredList that matches the user’s choices.

---

## How .filter() works

<img src="https://images.code.org/289a762a14af21d0486b3b8c84da939f-image-1769453111297.png" alt="javascript of using .filter to go throught the opportunites data base looking at jobs" width="750px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

#### This code  

- #### Starts with an empty new array

- #### Goes through `opportunities` one item at a time

- #### For each `job`, runs your callback function

- #### If your function returns **true**, that job gets added to `filteredList`

- #### If it returns **false**, it’s skipped

- #### When it’s done, you get a new array (`filteredList`) with only the kept items

#### **Important:** `.filter()` does not change the original array. It returns a new one.

## What is 'job' in the code?

#### Inside the filter, job is just a variable name for “the current item.”

#### So when .filter() is running:

- #### first job is { role:"Park Cleanup", ... }

- #### then job is { role:"Tree Planting", ... }

- #### and so on through all 10

---

## What gets returned (the “keep it?” test)

#### The filter function does two checks:

<img src="https://images.code.org/6e9a65c0da7346962e9ce7027afd2fe0-image-1769453536357.png" alt="javascript of usingfiltering age and category choice" width="750px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

## What that return line means

- #### `matchesCategory` && `matchesAge` is only true if both are **true**.

- #### So a job only gets kept if it matches the category rule and the age rule.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>



## Next 

Let's put it all together in  Web Lab.
MARKDOWN
